  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Wed Apr 22 2026\n\n...TODAY AND TONIGHT...\nStrong southwest flow across the Great Basin today will move east\nover the Central Rockies overnight. Dry, southwest flow at the \nbase of the system will lead to warm and dry conditions today. \nLowland highs in the mid-80s is 5 to 10 degrees warmer than normal\nfor late April. Winds will become gusty (20-30 mph) along east \nslopes of mountains overnight, in particular NE and Central El \nPaso.\n\n...THURSDAY...\nJet streak over northern New Mexico will generate a 992mb lee\nsurface low over the OK/TX panhandle Thursday afternoon, allowing\nwinds to increase out of the west across S NM and W TX. Sustained\nspeeds 15 to 25 mph and gusts up to 40 mph are expected, below\nAdvisory criteria. Wouldn't be surprised to see patchy blowing\ndust and hazy conditions along the I-10 corridor.\n\n...FRIDAY AND SATURDAY...\nNo major concerns Friday/Saturday. Winds remain breezy (15 to 20\nmph) each afternoon, but conditions stay dry and warm.\n\n...SUNDAY...\nWindy conditions on Sunday will be the strongest of the week.\n80-knot jet streak crossing the Southern Rockies is depicted in\nboth GFS and ECMWF deterministic models. Wind and Dust Headlines \nmay be needed as this event approaches. Deeper 989mb surface low\nover the High Plains will allow for wind gusts in the 50-60 mph \nrange. Areas of blowing dust and low visibility is the main\nconcern.\n\n...NEXT WEEK...\nContinued breezy conditions almost every day, but nothing that\nsounds alarms next week. Fetch of subtropical moisture moving up\nout of Baja along with forcing from a Pacific low could lead to\nlow-end precipitation chances midweek. Not expecting very much,\nthough.",
